	(text "Fan power: a separate 12 V path\n- The current input can't carry it. The 12 V input (J101, 0.5 A polyfuse, 3 A SS34 diode) is sized for the electronics. Keep it for them.\n- Fan input: give the fans their own power input, fused at the bike end with a 10 A automotive blade fuse. There's a Mini-Fit Jr 2-pin model (5569-02A2) in your hardware folder. A 2-pin Mini-Fit Jr is rated about 9 A per pin with 16 AWG wire, which is tight for 8 A plus start-up current. A 4-pin Mini-Fit Jr with two pins for +12 V and two for GND gives margin.\n- Per-header fuse: put a polyfuse on each fan header, for example 1.5 A hold and 3 A trip in 1812 size. Then one shorted or stalled fan can't take down the other 7.\n- Bulk capacitor: about 100 µF, rated 35 V, on the fan rail near the headers, to absorb the fans' switching current and start-up current.\n- PCB: 8 A needs a trace about 6 mm wide on standard 1 oz copper (for roughly 10 °C temperature rise), or a copper pour, or 2 oz copper. Keep the fan ground return away from the MCU and ADC ground, and join them at one point near the fan input.\n- Bike supply (please check): 100 W continuous, more at start-up, is a real load for the Ego's 12 V converter. Check that converter's rating.\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.748 22.352 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "f2ad54e1-a148-4918-99fb-b8965100b4f7")
	)
	(text "2x FAN output"
